            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous Membranous Dysmenorrhea in an Adolescent Girl: A Case Report and Literature Review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e139-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpag.2014.11.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1083-3188(14)00374-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Membranous dysmenorrhea is a rare entity. It involves the sloughing of the endometrium in 1 cylindrical or membranous piece, retaining the shape of the uterine cavity. Herein, we report the first case of spontaneous membranous dysmenorrhea in an adolescent girl.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 17-year-old girl was admitted to the emergency clinic with severe painful menstrual bleeding and passage of tissue via the vagina. Bloody endometrial tissue resembling the endometrial cavity expulsed from the vagina was seen on inspection. The pathologic diagnosis of the mass was membranous dysmenorrhea.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY AND CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">To our knowledge, this is the first case of the spontaneous occurrence of membranous dysmenorrhea. The relationship between membranous dysmenorrhea and endogenous or exogenous progesterone should be investigated further. A review of the literature on membranous dysmenorrhea is presented.</AbstractText>
